<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tavoliere delle Puglie</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tavoliere</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tavoliere delle Puglie is a broad plain in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> around the city of Foggia, the second largest plain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after the Po valley. In antiquity it was the land of the Daunians, and in later centuries it was used mainly as winter pasture for flocks driven down from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a> mountains. Since the 20th century it has been one of Italy's chief wheat-growing areas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tavoliere-delle-puglie.jpg" alt="Tavoliere delle Puglie" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Attilios at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tavoliere.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
